{
  "id": "spark-literal-types",
  "engine": "spark",
  "engine_version": "4.3.0",
  "title": "Literals: `INT`, then `BIGINT`, then `DECIMAL`; `1.5` is a decimal, `1e0` a double",
  "verdict": "`1` int; `2147483648` bigint; `1.5` decimal(2,1); `1e0`, `1.5D` double; suffixes `Y S L F D BD`",
  "area": "frontend",
  "options": [
    "literal_types"
  ],
  "layer": "frontend",
  "constructs": [
    "literals"
  ],
  "settings": {
    "spark.sql.ansi.enabled": [
      true,
      false
    ]
  },
  "description": [
    "An integer literal is the narrowest of `INT`, `BIGINT` and `DECIMAL(p,0)` that holds it (`2147483648` is `bigint`, `9223372036854775808` `decimal(19,0)`). A literal with a point is a decimal (`1.5` is `decimal(2,1)`, `-0.0` `decimal(1,1)`), one with an exponent a `double` (`1e0`). Suffixes pick the type: `1Y` tinyint, `1S` smallint, `1L` bigint, `1.5F` float, `1.5D` double, `1BD` decimal. `NULL` is `void`; `array(1, 2L)` is `array<bigint>`.",
    "So `2147483647 + 1` is `INT` arithmetic and raises under ANSI."
  ],
  "reference": "Substrait literals carry their type; the frontends (the SQL lift, the Ibis producer) choose it, and the Spark writer casts each literal to its declared type.",
  "status": "no-finding",
  "evidence": "probe",
